WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.438 --> 00:00:10.320
We have just hit 2100,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.660
We are now facing very serious opponents,

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:19.000
And for those who have been following the speedrun and kind of watching it evolve, we've

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.380
basically hit a rating where opening theory has become ultra important,

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.920
So what you'll see in the last couple of videos is that we spend a good amount of the time

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:35.360
we have during the game and the time that we analyze actually focusing on the opening

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:36.880
theory and trying to get it right,

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:42.640
Because if you're at 2100 standard on chess,com, like at this point you have to have a very

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.920
well put together opening repertoire in which you basically know your main lines,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:52.140
And the entire premise of the speedrun is that my explanations as well as the analysis

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:56.780
is made to the level at which we're at,

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:01.240
So if you're a newer chess player or if you're a beginner, hopefully you'll still find helpful

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.020
things within my explanations,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:10.840
But just realize that I'm trying to level the explanations at the rating range in which

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:12.520
we currently are located,

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:19.240
So having clarified that, let's throw ourselves into the pool,

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.800
And we probably will have black,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:22.560
We had white in our last speedrun game,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.120
It was an Alepyn, which our opponent played the E65 variation, was a pretty quick game,

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:31.400
Let's see what the chess gods have in store for us here,

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.400
There we go,

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.360
We have white again against Kazeo 2088,

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:38.480
And as usual, we begin with one E4,

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:41.080
Let's see what our opponent faces us with,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:47.120
And we have a French, which I think, believe it or not, we have not faced yet in this speedrun,

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:48.120
Don't quote me on that,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:49.120
I might be wrong,

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:55.000
Obviously, our response is D4, and here we are already at an early crossroads,

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:00.080
Now the line that I have recommended in previous speedruns is the advanced French, but not

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:04.880
just a regular advanced French, but rather the Niemcewicz variation of the advanced

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.920
French, where you go E5, black plays C5, and then you don't play the sort of automatic

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:10.920
move C3,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.920
Instead, you actually develop your knight,

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:18.380
It's a super counterintuitive line if you've never seen it before, because white voluntarily

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:24.600
gives away the pawn chain and allows C takes D4, and there's like a deep positional underpinning

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:29.840
to the Niemcewicz variation that makes it, I think, a very dangerous line for the unprepared

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:30.840
player,

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:35.880
But since this is a more opening theory minded speedrun, I want to play the line that I am

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:39.880
most comfortable with personally, the line that I've played for most of my life, and

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:45.720
that is the tarash variation, which is characterized by the move knight to D2,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:52.560
So if you know nothing about the French, then very briefly white has a couple of ways to

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.560
meet the move,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:58.120
D5, the most natural of white's responses and the most popular move is knight to C3,

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:02.920
That leads to incredibly theoretical variations, and that's something we don't want to touch

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.240
with a 10 foot pull,

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:08.280
There's also the exchange variation, which we also don't want to touch with a 10 foot

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:09.280
pull,

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:13.320
I think the tarash is a great line because it straddles the bridge between not being

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:21.560
too theoretical, but also having that venom which can allow you to really crush a player

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.960
who's not incredibly well prepared,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:28.000
In response to the tarash, black has two main continuations,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:33.480
The one that appears more commonly in modern day grandmaster chess is C5, and I'll talk

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.360
a little bit about it after the game,

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:40.720
The sort of more traditional French spirited move is knight to F6, and in response we push

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.640
E5, and we get kind of the advanced French structure,

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.720
Now the main line is bishop to D3, we bring our bishop out to the most natural square,

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:54.320
black plays C5, and here in fact we do play the move C3,

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.680
And you shouldn't think of this as an advanced variation,

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:02.880
This is the pawn structure that you will find in a lot of different French lines, but the

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:07.280
advanced variation has very, very different piece configurations for both sides,

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:12.320
So there's actually very little in common between this line of the tarash and the advanced

00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:16.560
French, by which I mean 3E5,

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.520
And in this position, white is at a further crossroads,

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.440
We have to defend the d4 pawn, and we can do it in two main ways,

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.400
The traditional move is to bring the knight out to E2, and there's a lot of positional

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:32.620
details that kind of stand behind the reason why a certain move is played, or we choose

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.240
one particular way of defending the d4 pawn,

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:38.840
But the move that I have always played, and I've played this with great success, the more

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:41.680
tactical line is knight G to F3,

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:46.320
So 10 to 15 years ago, maybe even 20 years ago, this was still a newer move that was

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:52.000
catching French players off guard, and there was a period where even the strongest GMs

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:57.820
and noted French experts were struggling tremendously against the move knight GF3,

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:02.360
But now this is a very well-known line, it's still less popular than knight to E2, but

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:07.520
in my opinion, it's more dangerous, and it's easier to learn and to play for the sort of

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.080
intermediate player,

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:14.680
So black responds with one of the many possible continuations, just kind of a regular developing

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:17.240
move, bishop to E7, and we do the same,

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.320
We castle king side, and here black is at a further crossroads,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:23.400
There is a super tactical line where black plays G5,

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:29.040
I'm really hoping that we face that line in this game, because that will allow me to illustrate

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.280
a lot of the juicy ideas that white has in this variation of the tarash,

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:40.680
But French players who aren't too familiar with the knight GF3 variation often resort

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:45.600
to the more natural continuation, which is just a castle king side, and in that position

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:51.040
white has sort of a small but very stable advantage, and white's play in that line is

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:55.080
pretty intuitive and rather unremarkable,

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.080
So our opponent is taking a little bit of time,

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:01.880
Don't misinterpret my words,

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:06.760
The tarash is still a very, very theoretical line, and you still have to know a lot of

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:12.200
theory if you want to play this line successfully for white, but you still can't compare the

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:16.800
amount of theory in the tarash to the amount of theory in three knight C3, which I would

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.120
never recommend, even to an advanced player, because the time investment that you have

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:28.120
to put in to play knight C3 successfully I just think is not realistic for most people

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.400
with a job and a family,

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.560
I think knight D2, again, kind of strikes that balance,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:39.200
It gives you a serious chance to fight for the advantage without being ridiculously theoretical,

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.040
There's a lot to know, but you can definitely learn this, and you can play the tarash very

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.040
successfully,

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:50.600
So we get back to this position, and we're facing the move A5, which is yet another of

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:53.920
black's kind of top-rate continuations,

00:06:53.920 --> 00:07:00.480
This move is very, very common in this line, and it's a more positional way for black to

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:01.520
treat this line,

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:05.220
Black is kind of grabbing space on the queen side,

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:09.680
Black is also preventing, well, knight B3 was impossible anyway because of C4,

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.800
Black's main idea is to drive this pawn down to A4 and make it very difficult for white

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:18.160
to reshuffle his pieces on the queen side, right?

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:24.320
That pawn on A4 really clamps down on some key queen side squares, and it makes it hard

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:26.320
for us to play B3,

00:07:26.320 --> 00:07:32.880
It covers the B3 square, and often black accompanies the move A5 with the follow of queen to B6,

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.200
which exerts even more pressure on the D4 pawn,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.760
So there's many ways that we could meet the move A5, but the most natural one, and the

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:48.600
one that I've played for most of my life, is just to play A4 and to freeze the A5 pawn

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:50.120
in its tracks,

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.920
I think this is a very unpleasant move for most French players because we're preventing

00:07:53.920 --> 00:07:59.520
black from achieving their main aim, that is to drive the pawn to A4,

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.600
So once again, we just shut all that garbage down, we play A4,

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:09.320
I've had this position in numerous over-the-board games, and here black plays pawn takes D4,

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:14.560
Our opponent displays a good knowledge of opening theory, the point of this sequence

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:21.080
is that by provoking us into playing A4, black has created an outpost for his knight or his

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.720
bishop, but typically his knight on B4,

00:08:23.720 --> 00:08:27.520
And we're probably going to see our opponent play the move knight to B4,

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.920
And I think one thing that has to happen as you climb the raiding ladder is you have to

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:38.600
have a more nuanced positional perspective on whatever situation you're in,

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:42.240
So I think a newer player would see the B4 square and say, well, this is really unpleasant,

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:43.240
right?

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:46.520
Black can play knight to B4 and attack our bishop, and that's a huge outpost for the

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:47.520
knight,

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:54.280
But the outpost on B4 is just one of many, many components in this super rich position,

00:08:54.280 --> 00:08:58.640
Some of those components favor black, but a lot of those components actually favor white,

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:02.800
So for one thing, we have the equivalent outpost on B5,

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:07.480
And it's true that it is a lot harder for us to get a knight to B5, because for us to

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:10.840
get a knight to B5, we would have to burn three tempi,

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:16.480
And you actually do see white playing the move knight B1 followed by knight C3 and sometimes

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:18.320
knight B5 in this line,

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:22.080
And the reason that you have these tempi to burn is that the center is closed,

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:27.480
And as I've clarified on many occasions, when the center is closed, you typically have more

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.560
time to do what it is that you want to do, whatever it is,

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:36.080
Like any maneuver, any plan, you have more time to achieve it because there's kind of

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:37.720
less going on,

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:41.840
And as long as you can keep your center defended, you've got quite a bit more time than you

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.120
would with a wide open center,

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:45.880
But I don't really want to play knight B1,

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:49.000
I don't want to commit to a particular mode of play,

00:09:49.000 --> 00:09:52.480
I want to wait for black to put his cards on the table,

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:59.760
And in these positions, white has quite a few moves that are non-committal, but that

00:09:59.760 --> 00:10:04.960
improve your position and that set ourselves up for success in the event that the center

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.080
opens up,

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.200
And if you're a more habitual French player with either side, you should know that there

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:13.080
is one idea that you should always be prepared for,

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:16.760
Like when we look at this position, we've talked about ways in which black can attack

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:18.160
the d4 pawn,

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:21.320
And in reality, there's only one such way black can play queen to B6,

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:25.320
But you can also attack this pawn chain from the other side, and it's a lot more common

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:28.600
across all French lines for black to play the move f6,

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.040
This opens up the center, it opens up the f-file,

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:35.760
And whatever we do, we need to be prepared in the event that black decides to play f6,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:41.720
And so a very, very good way for us to do that is just to situate the rook on e1,

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:45.800
Might seem like a pointless move because we're putting the rook on a closed file, but in

00:10:45.800 --> 00:10:50.280
light of my explanation, you should begin to understand how it helps us prepare against

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:51.280
f6,

00:10:51.280 --> 00:10:54.280
We're discouraging that move quite strongly because we take on f6,

00:10:54.280 --> 00:10:59.800
And not only does the rook then find itself on an open file, but the pawn on e6 is right

00:10:59.800 --> 00:11:01.800
in its crosshairs,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:07.960
And if after f6, e takes f6, black is forced to take back with a knight, then black weakens

00:11:07.960 --> 00:11:13.440
his grip on the e5 square, and we can use that juicy, juicy square as a big outpost

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:15.060
for our knight,

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:19.280
So black plays knight b4, our opponent obviously does not resist the temptation, and forces

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:22.880
us to decide on a spot for our bishop,

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:25.160
So there's a couple of ways that we could approach this,

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:28.340
We could stick our bishop on the equivalent outpost on b5,

00:11:28.340 --> 00:11:32.680
But I don't like that move as much because I like to play this line in a very tactical

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:34.160
attacking manner,

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:38.920
And so I really like the placement of this bishop on the b1 h7 diagonal,

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:39.920
Why do I say that?

00:11:39.920 --> 00:11:42.400
Because, well, the bishop is targeting the h7 pawn,

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:45.480
So there's Greek gift sacrifices that could happen,

00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:52.780
Once we bring more pieces to the king side, that bishop is going to be an invaluable asset

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:56.600
as we transition our attention from the center to the king side,

00:11:56.600 --> 00:12:00.720
And so therefore, the move that I like is bishop back to b1,

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:02.240
Nothing wrong with making a retreating move,

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:05.360
The bishop is just as effective here as it was on d3,

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:07.760
It's still aiming on h7,

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:12.920
And one additional idea that this move makes possible for later is rook a1 to a3,

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:17.200
We could actually lift the rook right under the knight's nose,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:21.900
Because in playing knight b4, black has cut his control over the a3 square,

00:12:21.900 --> 00:12:27.680
So in a future where, let's say, we move the knight away from f3, we could play rook a3

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:30.040
and quickly involve the rook into the attack,

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:36.200
So if you're looking at this from the perspective of space, it may seem like black is doing

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:37.200
fantastic again,

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.520
Like, he's played knight b4 and he's pushed our bishop to the back rank,

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:42.960
But that's why bishops exist,

00:12:42.960 --> 00:12:44.440
They're super long range pieces,

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:49.320
And I'm telling you, we're only a couple of moves away from being able to launch a mating

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:50.320
attack,

00:12:50.320 --> 00:12:53.560
And I'm telling you this very, very accurately,

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:59.520
I feel like we are only a couple of moves away from smashing through on the king side,

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:02.160
which could be hard to conceptualize,

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:06.960
But hopefully, the course of the game, if it goes in our favor, will allow me to demonstrate

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:07.960
that,

00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:11.240
OK, so our opponent continues to think,

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:16.640
Our opponent has a couple of issues that they still have to solve,

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:21.040
Obviously, there's this famous French bishop on c8, which still doesn't have a home,

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:27.040
Most people here, I think, have the inclination to play b6 and try to deploy the bishop via

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:28.520
a6,

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:33.480
But even if black does all that, that bishop on a6 is still kind of out of the action,

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:38.880
OK, instead our opponent plays knight to b8, which is a regrouping that I've seen before,

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:43.160
I guess our opponent is heading for c6 with the knight and probably wants to leave the

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:44.980
d7 square open for the bishop,

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:51.720
But this is where we start assembling our pieces, not into like full attacking position,

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:55.720
but we basically assemble our pieces in the general vicinity of the king side,

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:59.800
And as crazy as it may seem for me to say this, we're already preparing for a mating

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:00.800
attack,

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:04.880
So there's one particular maneuver that you have to know in order to play this position

00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:05.880
successfully,

00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:10.760
And most people in the chat are indicating this move, which is pretty impressive to me,

00:14:10.760 --> 00:14:12.000
And that is the move knight f1,

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:16.640
If you play the Ruy Lopez, really if you're a 1e4 player, you should be familiar with

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:23.200
this maneuver because it's virtually universal across a large amount of different openings,

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:24.800
The Ruy Lopez is one of them,

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:26.960
And this opening, the Taras French, is another,

00:14:26.960 --> 00:14:32.360
It is incredibly common for white to maneuver the knight over to g3,

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:33.920
Now why do we do that?

00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:36.360
What exactly is the knight doing on g3?

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.580
Well, the reasoning is pretty simple,

00:14:38.580 --> 00:14:42.000
First of all, we're kind of unclogging the pipes a little bit,

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:46.440
The knight on d2 is in the way of essentially all of our pieces,

00:14:46.440 --> 00:14:47.440
It's in the way of the queen,

00:14:47.440 --> 00:14:49.120
It's in the way of the dark squared bishop,

00:14:49.120 --> 00:14:53.200
On g3, it's completely out of the way of the other minor pieces,

00:14:53.200 --> 00:14:56.760
But the knight can itself be used as a major attacking weapon,

00:14:56.760 --> 00:15:03.700
We can jump from g3 over to h5, and you can already see that certain sacrifices are starting

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:04.700
to loom in the air,

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:07.020
Like knight takes g7 could be possible,

00:15:07.020 --> 00:15:12.120
If black plays g6 in response, we could stick the knight on f6, and sometimes that move

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:17.680
is very strong, even if it sacrifices a pawn, because it gives us complete and total dark

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:19.080
squared domination,

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.920
So already in this position, we have multiple ways that we can continue the attack,

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:27.800
The rook a3 move that I had mentioned earlier is a viable candidate move,

00:15:27.800 --> 00:15:32.480
We could set that rook up in position so that when the knights move out of the way, it can

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:34.160
jump into g3,

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:39.360
But another very typical idea that you'll see in a lot of French books and a lot of

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:43.440
attacking manuals is the move h2, h4,

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:48.920
And if this move works out successfully, then after the game, I can show you countless examples

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:54.280
of this move leading to a decisive attack, again, across many different openings,

00:15:54.280 --> 00:15:58.120
That's the beauty of the attack that we're about to unroll,

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:02.880
I really feel like all of these moves are essentially universal, so your eyes shouldn't

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:08.800
glaze over just because you're not a 1e4 player or you don't face the French a lot,

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:14.880
These are important ideas for you to conceptualize, because they form the underpinning of successful

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:15.880
attacking chess,

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:18.640
Now, what the heck is the point of h4?

00:16:18.640 --> 00:16:20.040
And it's not what you might think,

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:26.080
Of course, we can advance this pawn two squares to h6, but that would actually serve to close

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:27.080
the king side up,

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:30.600
That is the last thing that we want, because it would allow black to play g6, and then

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:36.680
it would be very, very hard to unclog the kind of reverse triangle pawn formation that

00:16:36.680 --> 00:16:38.800
black would have set up,

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:44.320
A much more common follow-up to h4 is, of course, knight f3 to g5, so we're creating

00:16:44.320 --> 00:16:49.920
a stronghold for the knight to jump to g5, and once the knight jumps to g5, the diagonal

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:54.400
now opens up for the queen to leap into h5, so you can see how quickly the pieces start

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:58.600
coming into position, and there's often a point at which you have to sacrifice something,

00:16:58.600 --> 00:17:04.700
For example, if we're allowed to play knight g5, and black responds with h6, as you should

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:09.400
already understand, the point isn't then just to jump back to h3,

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:15.440
It's often to leave the knight on g5, so that if black takes it with the h pawn, the h file

00:17:15.440 --> 00:17:21.680
opens up, so a great follow-up would be, for instance, queen d1 to h5, or even queen d1

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:25.080
to g4, targeting the g7 pawn and x-raying the king,

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:29.120
Now, all of this is great, you might be looking at this and saying, well, I get it, we want

00:17:29.120 --> 00:17:32.560
to go knight g5, but what happens if black takes the pawn on h4?

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:38.680
And I will be honest, I haven't decided yet on a specific response to that move, but this

00:17:38.680 --> 00:17:42.760
is something I'll address in a little bit more depth after the game, assuming our opponent

00:17:42.760 --> 00:17:48.040
doesn't take that pawn, but there's a couple of important things to understand before we

00:17:48.040 --> 00:17:52.920
even address the actual point of the sacrifice, and the first is that in these types of middle

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:58.200
games, a single pawn almost never decides the outcome of the game,

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:04.120
Almost all of the pieces are still on the board, and so sacking an h pawn is not something

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:08.840
that you should think of in the same category as a queen sacrifice, or like a piece sacrifice,

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:09.840
right?

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:14.560
You should distinguish in your head pawn sacrifices from piece sacrifices, and I know that sounds

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:18.820
very banal, but a lot of players, they lump that into the same category, like they think

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:22.520
of h4 as a huge commitment, and if the attack doesn't work out, we lose,

00:18:22.520 --> 00:18:26.760
And on the vast majority of levels, that's absolutely not the case,

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:32.320
So one very simple thing that we could do after bishop takes h4 would have just been

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:36.960
to take the bishop and claim that we have the bishop pair and dark square control,

00:18:36.960 --> 00:18:41.860
So it's really not such a big investment or commitment to where you have to have a clear

00:18:41.860 --> 00:18:45.280
understanding of what you're doing if black takes the pawn,

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:49.020
We'll talk about that more after the game, because black has taken the game in a completely

00:18:49.020 --> 00:18:53.500
different direction, he's played f6, which I think is a pretty adequate response to h4,

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:57.680
Black tries to open up the center and make it harder for us to concentrate our forces

00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:59.120
on the king side,

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:02.440
Well, we don't have too much of a choice here,

00:19:02.440 --> 00:19:05.260
We don't want to allow black to be the one to take on e5,

00:19:05.260 --> 00:19:07.620
We should do it from a position of strength,

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:10.700
So let's start by playing e takes f6,

00:19:10.700 --> 00:19:15.160
It would be great if we could play queen d1 to h5 here, unfortunately that is illegal,

00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:16.640
And now we reach a critical moment,

00:19:16.640 --> 00:19:22.200
Here we have to be very concrete and very accurate because we've got a weakness on t4

00:19:22.200 --> 00:19:23.800
and we have to keep that pawn in mind,

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:25.400
That's not just any pawn,

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:29.500
If we give that pawn up and black takes it with the bishop, then the f2 pawn will be

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:32.280
in the crosshairs of the rook and the bishop as well,

00:19:32.280 --> 00:19:35.480
So knight g5 might come to mind as a very tempting move,

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:39.460
But in response to knight g5, black will probably go h6,

00:19:39.460 --> 00:19:45.680
And if we just blindly throw our pieces onto the king side, we might very well end up at

00:19:45.680 --> 00:19:50.000
a dead end because black will take on d4 and generate pretty massive counterplay based

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:51.680
on an attack on f2,

00:19:51.680 --> 00:19:56.380
So if we do play knight g5, we actually have to calculate some variations there,

00:19:56.380 --> 00:20:00.760
Some of you are pointing out knight g5, h6, and then knight takes e6,

00:20:00.760 --> 00:20:04.980
But then black plays bishop takes e6, rook takes e6, and bishop takes d4,

00:20:04.980 --> 00:20:09.740
And again, it appears that white is at a dead end because black has major, major pressure

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:10.740
on f2,

00:20:10.740 --> 00:20:12.480
Then h4 is also going to be hanging,

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.680
That doesn't seem to be really in the spirit of the line,

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:19.000
So I'm going to take a little moment here and think about our candidate moves,

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:23.400
What's interesting is that we haven't really made any tactical commitments, right?

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:28.440
We haven't committed to playing sacrificially or even playing tactically,

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.580
We could take this in a positional direction,

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:36.320
Because if we think about the move f6 in sort of a positional framework, the main drawback

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:40.580
of this move is that it creates a massive hole on e5,

00:20:40.580 --> 00:20:45.880
And this hole could be used later on in the game as an outpost for our knight,

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:47.520
So you might say, well, what are we waiting for?

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:48.720
Let's go knight e5,

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:52.920
But if you go knight e5 too soon, this goes back to something that we discussed in an

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:56.800
earlier game, which is that you don't want to occupy a weak square with a pawn,

00:20:56.800 --> 00:20:59.320
You typically want to occupy it with a piece,

00:20:59.320 --> 00:21:02.520
And after knight takes e5, we would have to recapture with the pawn,

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:04.840
And we would also give up h4,

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.480
So you might say, well, how do we prepare the move knight e5?

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:09.000
Let's play bishop f4,

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:10.700
And that would be a totally viable move,

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:15.740
But I think a more challenging move for black would actually be bishop c1 to g5,

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:21.920
This is a more active way to try to remove one of black's critical defenders on the e5

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:22.920
square,

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:24.280
And this is a typical concept,

00:21:24.280 --> 00:21:28.560
So notice that we played h4 with a tactical aim in mind,

00:21:28.560 --> 00:21:30.840
We wanted to checkmate black with knight g5,

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:33.700
But we're actually using that move for positional purposes,

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:38.280
And this is the flexibility that I talk about really often, which is necessary if you want

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:40.040
to thrive at a high level,

00:21:40.040 --> 00:21:42.480
You can't get fixated on one particular goal,

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:44.560
OK, black did something unexpected,

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:46.880
So we change our frame of thinking,

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:50.680
And now we're playing very positionally for control over the e5 square,

00:21:50.680 --> 00:21:52.200
What is black likely to do here?

00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:56.680
Well, if black plays bishop takes g5, I actually think we could have some really, really cool

00:21:56.680 --> 00:22:03.360
tactics there based on a Greek gift sacrifice with bishop takes h7, believe it or not,

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:07.000
And we'll explore that after the game because I don't think black is going to take our bishop,

00:22:07.000 --> 00:22:10.080
But it essentially functions like a regular Greek gift,

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:15.220
Bishop g5, bishop h7, king h7, we recapture on g5 with check,

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:18.160
And after king g8, our queen drives into h5,

00:22:18.160 --> 00:22:23.440
And I feel like the attack is unstoppable, much like it is in a regular Greek gift sacrifice,

00:22:23.440 --> 00:22:25.360
So what happens if black doesn't take on g5?

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:27.520
Well, the move I'm expecting is h6,

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:31.740
I think this is going to be the instinct of most 2,000 players,

00:22:31.740 --> 00:22:34.480
And then, of course, we play bishop takes bishop,

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:40.240
Black will probably recapture with the queen, which I think is the most natural way of recapturing,

00:22:40.240 --> 00:22:45.240
And then I think would be a great time to increase our control over the e5 square by

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:50.480
applying the rook lifts that I had talked about earlier, like rook a3, rook e3,

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:55.680
And again, when I had mentioned this rook lift many moves ago, I mentioned it in a tactical

00:22:55.680 --> 00:22:56.680
context,

00:22:56.680 --> 00:23:00.480
I mentioned it in the context of trying to get the rook, let's say, to the g file,

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:05.080
And again, we're repurposing a tactical idea into a positional idea,

00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:10.480
We're using the rook lift to increase our control over this key central square on e5,

00:23:10.480 --> 00:23:16.840
And we're going to try to strangle black positionally rather than checkmate him tactically if h6

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:17.840
happens,

00:23:17.840 --> 00:23:21.400
Now, you might hear all of that and say, OK, this makes sense,

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:24.960
But haven't we seriously weakened our king side with h4?

00:23:24.960 --> 00:23:28.000
And h4 is far from a serious weakening of the king side,

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:33.520
It really isn't a big deal that we pushed the h-bond, because we still have the core

00:23:33.520 --> 00:23:37.920
of our king side pawn formation, and our king is still perfectly safe,

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:42.400
Queen c7, OK, that's a decent move, I think, by our opponent, which doesn't seem to do

00:23:42.400 --> 00:23:43.400
all that much,

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:47.580
I guess it unpins the bishop, but it's not like the bishop has all that much mobility

00:23:47.580 --> 00:23:49.000
to begin with,

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:54.140
And I think there's no need for us to take on f6 and allow black's rook to jump into

00:23:54.140 --> 00:23:55.720
a pretty good square on f6,

00:23:55.720 --> 00:23:59.960
I think we should proceed with our plan as if black didn't do anything,

00:23:59.960 --> 00:24:02.960
And we already know what a great way to improve our position is,

00:24:02.960 --> 00:24:05.040
I think we should play rook a3,

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:08.160
And we start playing this kind of general waiting game, where both of us are trying

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:12.040
to improve our position, and eventually things are going to clarify,

00:24:12.040 --> 00:24:17.380
Something is going to happen very, very soon, because the tension has reached a peak,

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:21.320
But for the time being, we can just chill, because everything is nicely defended,

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:25.140
One annoying aspect of this position is that we can't put our queen on d3 or c2,

00:24:25.140 --> 00:24:26.600
That would be incredible,

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:27.600
That would be sexy,

00:24:27.600 --> 00:24:29.120
That would be like the best battery of all time,

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:32.600
So we have to give some credit to the placement of this knight on b4,

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:38.000
It is restricting the mobility of our queen, but we can do without it,

00:24:38.000 --> 00:24:42.780
And our bishop is still an excellent piece, because it's still creating constant headaches

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:45.000
for black on the h7 square,

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:51.160
The rook a3, lifting the rook up to e3, and you shouldn't think of that move as increasing

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:55.880
the pressure on the pawn, because the pawn on e6 is actually pretty well defended by

00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:56.880
the bishop,

00:24:56.880 --> 00:25:00.560
So unless we attack it with a minor piece, that pawn is not going to be winnable,

00:25:00.560 --> 00:25:06.280
Instead, we're increasing our control over that stronghold on e5, and one day we hope

00:25:06.280 --> 00:25:07.920
to occupy that square with the knight,

00:25:07.920 --> 00:25:09.600
We don't know when that day will arrive,

00:25:09.600 --> 00:25:12.880
We don't know if that's ultimately going to be what wins us the game,

00:25:12.880 --> 00:25:16.640
But by doubling rooks on the e-file, we're actually making it very difficult for black

00:25:16.640 --> 00:25:18.040
to breathe,

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:24.160
So already black is, I think, starting to run out of helpful moves,

00:25:24.160 --> 00:25:28.760
When that happens, usually people try to force things, so I'm expecting h6,

00:25:28.760 --> 00:25:33.720
And in response to h6, we probably will take the bishop on f6,

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:37.760
Another thing that you should be aware of is that if we play bishop takes bishop, we

00:25:37.760 --> 00:25:41.980
should not be afraid of black taking back with the pawn,

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:46.720
Because that fixes one problem, right, of the e5 score, but it creates like 50 others,

00:25:46.720 --> 00:25:51.160
If black plays g takes f6, the king is going to be wide open, and the pawn on h6 is going

00:25:51.160 --> 00:25:56.080
to be a sitting duck, and we can simply target it by playing queen to d2 in that position,

00:25:56.080 --> 00:26:01.440
You should always remember the possibility of leaping into h5 with your knight, right?

00:26:01.440 --> 00:26:06.160
If black ever brings the king up to g7, that knight from g3 can jump into h5 and force

00:26:06.160 --> 00:26:07.640
the king right back to g8,

00:26:07.640 --> 00:26:12.320
Okay, so obviously black plays rook e8, we play rook e3, and the kind of waiting game

00:26:12.320 --> 00:26:13.780
continues,

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:16.560
This is where things are about to get very exciting,

00:26:16.560 --> 00:26:19.640
So Lamamu asks, what if black plays e5?

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:24.040
I haven't calculated this move, but I'll bet you a silver dollar that e5 is never going

00:26:24.040 --> 00:26:25.840
to work in this position,

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:26.840
How do I know that?

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:30.780
Because black's position is so riddled with holes and so passive, that opening up the

00:26:30.780 --> 00:26:33.520
center like that just isn't going to work,

00:26:33.520 --> 00:26:38.640
In response to e5, we can, for instance, take on f6, and after rook takes f6, pawn takes

00:26:38.640 --> 00:26:43.520
e5, we literally have more defenders on the e5 square than black has attackers, so it

00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:45.080
just loses a pawn,

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:50.320
So you should, I mean, e5 is a legitimate idea in the French, but in this particular

00:26:50.320 --> 00:26:54.480
situation, we're so much better prepared for the center to be open, that this isn't something

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:56.760
that's even on my radar,

00:26:56.760 --> 00:26:58.100
Queen d6,

00:26:58.100 --> 00:27:01.760
So to our opponent's credit, he is sort of maintaining his composure, which means we

00:27:01.760 --> 00:27:05.600
need to turn the pressure dial up a little bit more,

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:08.700
Now we can make moves belonging to two categories here,

00:27:08.700 --> 00:27:13.340
We can continue playing like general Russian schoolboy moves, and that would involve queen

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:19.360
to d2, or even a move like b3, you'll sometimes see GMs play to shore up the a4 pawn, kind

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:21.000
of pass the ball back to black,

00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:26.320
But I feel personally that the time has come for us to take more concrete, more decisive

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:29.680
action, because we've essentially improved our position to the maximum,

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:34.480
And there is one move, there is one asset that we haven't exploited yet, which is of

00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:38.340
course the knight jump to h5, and this is what we just talked about,

00:27:38.340 --> 00:27:42.440
I think this would be a great opportunity for us to throw that move in,

00:27:42.440 --> 00:27:44.400
Why are we playing knight h5 here?

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:46.880
Because you might look at this and say, well, I don't see what this threatens,

00:27:46.880 --> 00:27:49.380
Well, this doesn't threaten anything tactical,

00:27:49.380 --> 00:27:53.360
We're not threatening to win a piece, but what we are threatening to do is completely

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:59.560
ruin black's pawn cover by taking this bishop and forcing black to take back with a pawn,

00:27:59.560 --> 00:28:03.760
In particular, this is bad for black because then the bishop can also jump into h6, and

00:28:03.760 --> 00:28:08.000
the dark squares around black's king are going to be completely indefensible,

00:28:08.000 --> 00:28:11.400
So that's like an unthinkable option for black,

00:28:11.400 --> 00:28:17.000
We're essentially forcing black to take on g5, and we already know that this trade, particularly

00:28:17.000 --> 00:28:20.600
in that version, is super favorable for us,

00:28:20.600 --> 00:28:26.700
A, because the e5 square will then be under our complete control, and B, because tactically

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:32.640
after bishop takes g5, we could consider either a Greek gift sacrifice on h7, which honestly

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:37.440
is a little bit less appealing to me, because the h5 square is occupied by the knight, or

00:28:37.440 --> 00:28:42.240
in that situation, we could have just recaptured with the pawn and then stuck a knight on e5,

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:47.740
King h8, our opponent calls our bluff, isn't afraid of knight takes f6, and actually prepares

00:28:47.740 --> 00:28:51.280
for that eventuality by opening up the g8 square for the rook,

00:28:51.280 --> 00:28:56.440
So he's sort of preemptively tucking the king away so that knight takes f6 comes with a

00:28:56.440 --> 00:29:00.680
little bit less force, which means that we should kind of consider changing our plans,

00:29:00.680 --> 00:29:06.600
I no longer think knight takes f6 is the best way to squeeze the most out of the position,

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:11.600
because after gf, it's actually not clear who that transformation favors, because once

00:29:11.600 --> 00:29:14.080
black puts a rook on g8, guess what?

00:29:14.080 --> 00:29:18.720
Our king isn't the safest piece either, and black could generate some counterplay,

00:29:18.720 --> 00:29:23.120
So now I think would be a good time to ask ourselves, do we want to do something tactical?

00:29:23.120 --> 00:29:26.200
Do we want to try to checkmate black on the king side, or do we want to continue improving

00:29:26.200 --> 00:29:27.580
our position?

00:29:27.580 --> 00:29:32.160
Let me think about this for just a second, because this is an important moment,

00:29:32.160 --> 00:29:36.680
I have an idea that I think is a good move, but I have to double check,

00:29:36.680 --> 00:29:41.360
Yeah, I'm pretty sure that the move I have in mind actually might win the game,

00:29:41.360 --> 00:29:45.920
So remember that I mentioned earlier that when we have a weak square, we don't want

00:29:45.920 --> 00:29:47.760
to occupy it with a pawn,

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:50.680
Well, there's an asterisk and a caveat to that, right?

00:29:50.680 --> 00:29:55.960
Because everything I say of that form is not a rule, right?

00:29:55.960 --> 00:30:00.160
It's a general guideline, and the situation on the board could change your approach to

00:30:00.160 --> 00:30:01.160
that guideline,

00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:05.600
So I think that the best move here and the best way to squeeze the most out of the position

00:30:05.600 --> 00:30:07.940
is in fact to play knight to e5,

00:30:07.940 --> 00:30:12.200
Now this is based on both a positional and tactical observation,

00:30:12.200 --> 00:30:16.360
Tactically it's easy to observe that if black takes with the knight, yeah, we take back

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:17.960
with the pawn, but this is a fork,

00:30:17.960 --> 00:30:21.200
This forks the queen and bishop and wins a minor piece,

00:30:21.200 --> 00:30:22.680
But what about bishop takes e5?

00:30:22.680 --> 00:30:27.640
Well, in that case, we take back with the pawn, and I didn't calculate any further

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:28.640
than that,

00:30:28.640 --> 00:30:33.520
I calculated up to that position, and I concluded that the presence of two minor pieces on perfect

00:30:33.520 --> 00:30:39.160
attacking positions, the possibility of us completing our rook lift to g3, and the ease

00:30:39.160 --> 00:30:43.300
with which we can involve our queen in the attack basically means that black's situation

