So that's the reason why you have to be okay with sacking the pawn, and the way to do that

00:46:46.080 --> 00:46:48.080
is by castling king side,

00:46:48.080 --> 00:46:50.720
So after queen b6, the main move is to castle king side,

00:46:50.720 --> 00:46:53.640
Black, of course, takes the pawn in the vast majority of cases,

00:46:53.640 --> 00:46:56.880
Blah, blah, blah, blah, takes, takes, takes, takes,

00:46:56.880 --> 00:47:03.280
Now the knight from d2 jumps into the game with knight to f3, hits the queen, protects

00:47:03.280 --> 00:47:04.280
the pawn,

00:47:04.280 --> 00:47:07.340
The queen moves back to b6, this is the main line,

00:47:07.340 --> 00:47:13.600
Now the modern engines have come up with a really nice way of playing this line with

00:47:13.600 --> 00:47:14.600
white,

00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:20.360
So traditionally, the main move was queen to a4, trying to exert some pressure on this

00:47:20.360 --> 00:47:21.360
knight,

00:47:21.360 --> 00:47:26.880
But recently, Stockfish came up with, I think, a much cooler idea, which is to play a3,

00:47:26.880 --> 00:47:29.160
And the idea of a3 is quite simple,

00:47:29.160 --> 00:47:32.560
Let's say the black develops his bishop with bishop to e7,

00:47:32.560 --> 00:47:37.500
First of all, a3 discourages black from developing the bishop to c5, because this runs right

00:47:37.500 --> 00:47:39.520
into b4,

00:47:39.520 --> 00:47:44.560
So typically black develops the bishop to e7 in the French, and now the point is that

00:47:44.560 --> 00:47:46.960
white has the move bishop to e3,

00:47:46.960 --> 00:47:50.420
This is an important idea to understand,

00:47:50.420 --> 00:47:53.720
Now what happens if black plays queen takes b2, who can tell me?

00:47:53.720 --> 00:47:56.340
Why is the move a3 necessary?

00:47:56.340 --> 00:48:03.000
This is like my analysis from pretty recently, so you'll get an inside perspective of what

00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:05.480
I myself play against the French,

00:48:05.480 --> 00:48:06.800
Hopefully you'll like these ideas,

00:48:06.800 --> 00:48:10.840
Yeah, so of course you play bishop d4, and the queen is trapped,

00:48:10.840 --> 00:48:12.480
So why was a3 necessary?

00:48:12.480 --> 00:48:17.120
Because it covers the b4 square, otherwise black would be able to evacuate via b4,

00:48:17.120 --> 00:48:22.920
So in this situation, black cannot take on b2, cannot block with a knight because of

00:48:22.920 --> 00:48:29.080
b4, and so black must either satisfy himself with bishop to c5, which is in general a very

00:48:29.080 --> 00:48:32.160
undesirable move strategically in the French,

00:48:32.160 --> 00:48:36.360
And the reason why is that now the g5 square is uncovered,

00:48:36.360 --> 00:48:39.880
Now you might be like, g5 square is uncovered, why is that important?

00:48:39.880 --> 00:48:43.720
The reason it's important is because after a move like bishop to d2, which by the way

00:48:43.720 --> 00:48:45.760
is far from a bad move,

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:48.760
Let me just pull up my file just a moment,

00:48:48.760 --> 00:48:53.760
What can you notice in the event the black castles kingside?

00:48:53.760 --> 00:48:58.380
Well with the g5 square uncovered, the Greek gift sacrifice is now possible,

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:02.620
After king takes h7, knight g5, it's a classic Greek gift, and it's a terrible version for

00:49:02.620 --> 00:49:06.400
black because all of his pieces are scattered about on the queenside,

00:49:06.400 --> 00:49:12.520
So after bishop to c5, I actually think bishop d2 is the best move, and it is the best move

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:14.680
according to the engine,

00:49:14.680 --> 00:49:18.480
Black has no good moves, the bishop on c5 is stuck, and the Greek gift sacrifice works

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:21.840
really, really nicely in this version,

00:49:21.840 --> 00:49:28.600
The line goes on, black plays king to g6 here, white delivers a check on c2, forces black

00:49:28.600 --> 00:49:36.840
to play a5, and after ef6, king f6, I won't bore you with the crazy details of this line,

00:49:36.840 --> 00:49:42.220
but after queen h7, white has a massive attack, the rook is coming to e1, there's a million

00:49:42.220 --> 00:49:47.520
mate threats, and this is essentially unplayable for black from a practical standpoint,

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:49.320
So hopefully that makes sense,

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:51.460
That's why bishop c5 isn't good,

00:49:51.460 --> 00:49:57.860
So black essentially has to resort to playing queen back to d8, which is super passive,

00:49:57.860 --> 00:50:03.760
and now rather than trying to checkmate black in one go, the best approach here is just

00:50:03.760 --> 00:50:06.800
to play quiet, simple positional chess,

00:50:06.800 --> 00:50:12.560
You start by playing rook to e1, just like solidifying your control over the center,

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:17.120
typically black like castles kingside, now it's a great idea to bring your queen out

00:50:17.120 --> 00:50:23.200
to c2 and force a weakening move like h6, and now the engine likes rook a to d1, bringing

00:50:23.200 --> 00:50:27.240
the rest of the pieces into the center, and just look at black's position and how hard

00:50:27.240 --> 00:50:28.520
it is to play,

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:32.280
And one of the more beautiful ideas is if black tries to develop his queenside with

00:50:32.280 --> 00:50:35.920
b6, white already has a winning idea,

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:37.800
Why did we induce the move h6?

00:50:37.800 --> 00:50:39.520
Why is that a weakening move?

00:50:39.520 --> 00:50:41.880
Well, this is an idea that you should be familiar with,

00:50:41.880 --> 00:50:49.080
Who can tell me how white sets up an unstoppable threat of basically checkmate on the kingside?

00:50:49.080 --> 00:50:52.240
This is a really, really cool move, really cool move,

00:50:52.240 --> 00:50:56.240
So it's not g4, because g4 weakens white's own kingside,

00:50:56.240 --> 00:50:59.440
There is a much more effective way of exploiting this hook,

00:50:59.440 --> 00:51:01.920
Not bishop h7, rook d4, it's a retreating move,

00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:06.000
Yeah, so like the move you want to play is bishop h6, but if you play it too quickly,

00:51:06.000 --> 00:51:07.560
you don't have a follow up,

00:51:07.560 --> 00:51:09.440
So how do we ensure that there's a follow up?

00:51:09.440 --> 00:51:10.880
Yeah, queen c1,

00:51:10.880 --> 00:51:15.320
Best move is queen c1, because you're also keeping an eye on the c file,

00:51:15.320 --> 00:51:20.600
And if black plays, let's say bishop b7, then this move already works like a charm,

00:51:20.600 --> 00:51:25.200
Definitely something to file away into your like mental directory of tactical ideas,

00:51:25.200 --> 00:51:31.560
Like this position is already over, black has to try f5, and you want to pass on it,

00:51:31.560 --> 00:51:36.160
Black has to take with the knight, and here I think the fastest engine checkmate is to

00:51:36.160 --> 00:51:38.920
bring the rook up to e5 and then over to g5,

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:43.920
And again, notice that if black tries to escape, he gets checkmated in one move on g6,

00:51:43.920 --> 00:51:46.340
So after queen c1, black can essentially resign,

00:51:46.340 --> 00:51:49.600
Bishop h6 is completely unstoppable,

00:51:49.600 --> 00:51:54.320
The line I analyzed was knight c5, and even here you can play bishop takes h6,

00:51:54.320 --> 00:51:57.680
You actually don't need the light squared bishop, which is kind of cool,

00:51:57.680 --> 00:52:02.520
It shows you how abundant white's tactical ideas are,

00:52:02.520 --> 00:52:07.960
You don't need your light squared bishop, because here this is plenty, plenty sufficient,

00:52:07.960 --> 00:52:12.940
And one really, really pretty idea is, let's say bishop to d7,

00:52:12.940 --> 00:52:19.740
The best way to win is to go knight g5 and then rook g3, and rook takes g5 is unstoppable,

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:24.320
If black plays f6, there's a check, and there's a mate on h3,

00:52:24.320 --> 00:52:29.680
So just one of many, many lines that show you the tactical potential in white's position

00:52:29.680 --> 00:52:30.920
in this line,

00:52:30.920 --> 00:52:36.520
So this is partially why I recommend the taros, because it really helps you develop your tactical

00:52:36.520 --> 00:52:38.020
abilities,

00:52:38.020 --> 00:52:44.640
So after queen b6, the new move is a3, and I really think a3 poses major, major problem

00:52:44.640 --> 00:52:46.400
for black in this line,

00:52:46.400 --> 00:52:51.080
Of course, knight c5 is never scary, because you can just drop the bishop back to c2, and

