then your knight can come out to g5, your queen to h5, this is even worse for black,

00:52:56.080 --> 00:52:59.640
And the knight can be always chased away with the move b4,

00:52:59.640 --> 00:53:04.040
Sometimes French players like to go like this, they go knight c5 and a5, but here knight

00:53:04.040 --> 00:53:12.520
to g5 strikes at the exposed king side, and if h6, just watch how quickly the attack turns

00:53:12.520 --> 00:53:13.520
decisive,

00:53:13.520 --> 00:53:18.920
You go queen h5, and if black plays g6, the move should be totally automatic to you,

00:53:18.920 --> 00:53:23.080
If it takes g6, it's like all completely automatic,

00:53:23.080 --> 00:53:28.760
But an instructive application of the theme I mentioned at the end of our game comes in

00:53:28.760 --> 00:53:29.860
this position,

00:53:29.860 --> 00:53:32.160
What do you think is white's best move?

00:53:32.160 --> 00:53:33.680
What do you think is white's best move here?

00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:34.680
What is the winning move?

00:53:34.680 --> 00:53:39.160
Yeah, you don't go for mate, so a lot of newer players, they might sit there and, what about

00:53:39.160 --> 00:53:40.160
this check?

00:53:40.160 --> 00:53:41.940
Well, this check doesn't do anything,

00:53:41.940 --> 00:53:45.440
The winning move is just knight f7, the freaking rook is trapped,

00:53:45.440 --> 00:53:46.440
Literally the rook is trapped,

00:53:46.440 --> 00:53:50.760
We've only sacrificed one minor piece, and we've gotten two pawns for it,

00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:53.280
Well, I guess one pawn, because we were a pawn down,

00:53:53.280 --> 00:53:55.240
The rook cannot be defended,

00:53:55.240 --> 00:53:57.680
We're just going to be an exchange up,

00:53:57.680 --> 00:54:01.040
If black counterattacks the rook, big deal, we just go rook b1,

00:54:01.040 --> 00:54:04.400
So a very, very important takeaway from this game is that a lot of these attacks, they

00:54:04.400 --> 00:54:08.920
don't actually conclude in checkmate, they conclude in the gain of material,

00:54:08.920 --> 00:54:13.440
And if black plays queen c7, there's another classic idea, knight f7, queen f7, and bishop

00:54:13.440 --> 00:54:15.440
g6 pins the queen,

00:54:15.440 --> 00:54:21.040
So I could analyze this for several more hours, but it was important to me to demonstrate

00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:23.120
what to do after queen to b6,

00:54:23.120 --> 00:54:27.720
Our opponent chose the sort of second main line, bishop e7, and here there's only one

