other variation that I want to explore in depth,

00:54:30.840 --> 00:54:35.760
And once again, I will reveal some of my like deeply held opening secrets, and that is the

00:54:35.760 --> 00:54:37.160
move g5,

00:54:37.160 --> 00:54:42.520
So there was a point at which this line was considered to be essentially the refutation

00:54:42.520 --> 00:54:44.260
of the knight f3 variation,

00:54:44.260 --> 00:54:49.920
And if you're a French player, you know, you should be aware of this incredibly scary idea

00:54:49.920 --> 00:54:51.880
of playing g5,

00:54:51.880 --> 00:54:55.220
And what makes this idea scary is that black just wants to go g4,

00:54:55.220 --> 00:54:59.200
There are some versions in which like this move literally traps the knight,

00:54:59.200 --> 00:55:03.880
But of course, the larger point is to throw the knight off of f3, which releases white's

00:55:03.880 --> 00:55:06.240
control over the d4 pawn,

00:55:06.240 --> 00:55:11.320
So if white handles this in cautiously, then white's entire center can collapse,

00:55:11.320 --> 00:55:16.400
If white plays h3, this often exacerbates the problem, because now g4 will come and

00:55:16.400 --> 00:55:20.840
potentially open up the h file, and that's the last thing you want, okay?

00:55:20.840 --> 00:55:27.700
So when I faced this line for the first time about 15 years ago, I tried something interesting,

00:55:27.700 --> 00:55:33.240
After h5, I said, I'm going to stop the progress of black's pawns by playing g4,

00:55:33.240 --> 00:55:37.440
And in response to this, my opponent applied one of the most beautiful ideas that have

00:55:37.440 --> 00:55:40.200
ever been played against me in the French,

00:55:40.200 --> 00:55:41.960
Here's what happened in that game,

00:55:41.960 --> 00:55:47.120
My opponent, whose name was Abhijit Gupta, Indian Grandmaster, we got this position,

