And it was only after that game that I basically like went back to the drawing board and developed

00:55:56.560 --> 00:56:01.220
a really good system against g5, because I got smashed,

00:56:01.220 --> 00:56:03.920
So let me double check,

00:56:03.920 --> 00:56:07.920
Okay, it was a slightly different position,

00:56:07.920 --> 00:56:16.520
It was actually this position, except black's bishop was still on, oh, just a moment,

00:56:16.520 --> 00:56:20.240
It was a slightly different variation, but I'm pretty sure that this idea can be applied

00:56:20.240 --> 00:56:21.240
here,

00:56:21.240 --> 00:56:25.560
Basically, what black does is he plays queen b6,

00:56:25.560 --> 00:56:30.480
Now sacrificing the pawn here makes a lot less sense, because white's pawn is on g4

00:56:30.480 --> 00:56:32.000
and the king is so weak,

00:56:32.000 --> 00:56:36.600
So the only thing that white can do is play queen a4, and here the idea still applies,

00:56:36.600 --> 00:56:41.680
My opponent, in a very similar position, played the move a6,

00:56:41.680 --> 00:56:46.120
Now who can tell me what the heck the idea of this move is?

00:56:46.120 --> 00:56:50.920
The position that we actually reached was really, really similar,

00:56:50.920 --> 00:56:56.000
It was this exact position, but black's bishop was still on f8, in other words,

00:56:56.000 --> 00:56:59.880
Yeah, queen to a7 and b5,

00:56:59.880 --> 00:57:04.240
And it turns out that white's pawn on d4 collapses, because b5 is unstoppable,

00:57:04.240 --> 00:57:08.120
You're going to lose d4, and then you're going to lose d5, and you're going to lose the game,

00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:11.160
This is just crushing, a crushing idea,

00:57:11.160 --> 00:57:14.000
My opponent carried this idea out and won the game,

00:57:14.000 --> 00:57:18.800
So that's when I realized that you never, never, never want to meet this by playing

