00:57:22.800 --> 00:57:27.520
So instead, I came back and I developed an idea that I still think is very strong, and

00:57:27.520 --> 00:57:34.680
that is the move queen to e2, which is actually, I think, a brilliant idea,

00:57:34.680 --> 00:57:39.180
It's still not a very common move, so you're still likely to catch a lot of players by

00:57:39.180 --> 00:57:42.200
surprise with this move,

00:57:42.200 --> 00:57:45.600
Now first of all, let's understand what happens if black plays g4,

00:57:45.600 --> 00:57:48.640
So obviously, you drop the knight back to e1, and you might look at this and say, well,

00:57:48.640 --> 00:57:49.880
I don't get it,

00:57:49.880 --> 00:57:52.280
Doesn't this just help black collapse our center?

00:57:52.280 --> 00:57:55.720
Well, the caveat is that the g4 pawn is now hanging,

00:57:55.720 --> 00:58:01.060
And if black takes on d4, we recapture on g4, and this is a terrible version for black,

00:58:01.060 --> 00:58:08.400
because although black has succeeded in, essentially, exploding our center, black now has massive

00:58:08.400 --> 00:58:13.540
fatal weaknesses on the king side, because the queen is coming to g7,

00:58:13.540 --> 00:58:20.120
After knight to c6, the queen literally dives into g7, forces rook f8, and the e5 pawn

