direction for black, queen to b6, queen h5, check, and rook g3, et cetera,

01:11:30.120 --> 01:11:32.320
This is easily, easily winning,

01:11:32.320 --> 01:11:35.960
Just rook h3, and black has no pieces on the king side,

01:11:35.960 --> 01:11:41.840
So one of the sub-themes of this game is not all attacks are the same,

01:11:41.840 --> 01:11:46.920
And when you hear the word attack, you often associate it with a lightning series of moves

01:11:46.920 --> 01:11:49.520
where every move has to be a sacrifice,

01:11:49.520 --> 01:11:53.920
Many times when you're attacking, you can afford to play slowly because black has nothing

01:11:53.920 --> 01:11:55.440
going for him on the other side of the board,

01:11:55.440 --> 01:12:01.440
So a lot of attacks have to be conducted in this very slow or relatively slow and deliberate

01:12:01.440 --> 01:12:02.440
way,

01:12:02.440 --> 01:12:07.720
And the buildup to the sacrifice is the stage of attack that's not talked about enough,

01:12:07.720 --> 01:12:11.640
When you're solving exercises or you're reading books, they usually talk about the final

01:12:11.640 --> 01:12:12.840
stage,

01:12:12.840 --> 01:12:16.120
That's like the stage which begins with this move,

01:12:16.120 --> 01:12:20.240
But the stage that's not talked about enough is the one that involves moves like h4 and

01:12:20.240 --> 01:12:22.640
knight h5 and rook a3,

01:12:22.640 --> 01:12:28.800
Remember that those buildup moves are an integral part of every attack, particularly attacks

01:12:28.800 --> 01:12:31.240
that involve this many moving parts,

01:12:31.240 --> 01:12:37.280
So our opponent played f6, which I think relatively is maybe even the best resource we take on

01:12:37.280 --> 01:12:43.360
f6, and now a critical part of the game where we transition from attacking play to positional

01:12:43.360 --> 01:12:45.880
play with bishop to g5,

01:12:45.880 --> 01:12:51.640
So that's a long speech, but hopefully this helps you understand how white's attack actually

01:12:51.640 --> 01:12:52.780
works,

01:12:52.780 --> 01:12:57.440
And once again, in response to bishop takes g5, we have the Greek gift sacrifice,

01:12:57.440 --> 01:13:02.720
So notice how we're like seamlessly weaving between positional play and tactical play

01:13:02.720 --> 01:13:05.040
based on what black is doing,

01:13:05.040 --> 01:13:10.540
So if bishop g5, we deliver a game-winning tactic with bishop h7,

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:14.800
Our opponent played queen c7, and here we continue playing positionally with rook to

01:13:14.800 --> 01:13:20.960
a3, our goal to secure complete control of the e5 square,

01:13:20.960 --> 01:13:26.180
Now if black had played h6 and tried to force things, yeah, we would have taken on f6,

01:13:26.180 --> 01:13:30.200
And then we could have thrown in the move knight h5 because it doesn't hurt,

01:13:30.200 --> 01:13:36.460
And then we could have continued by dominating the e5 square with knight e5,

01:13:36.460 --> 01:13:39.800
And the attack flows naturally, now we stick a rook on e5, the other rook can come into

01:13:39.800 --> 01:13:46.340
g3, and we can kind of go back to deciding the game via attack,

01:13:46.340 --> 01:13:48.120
So h6 is never scary,

01:13:48.120 --> 01:13:51.800
Our opponent decided to keep the tension, and the rest of the game I think is pretty

01:13:51.800 --> 01:13:52.800
much natural,

01:13:52.800 --> 01:13:54.800
We go rook e3,

01:13:54.800 --> 01:14:00.060
I think the engine always finds ways that the defending side can survive for longer,

01:14:00.060 --> 01:14:05.140
but hopefully the course of the game was able to show you just how difficult it is for black

01:14:05.140 --> 01:14:07.360
to play a position like this,

01:14:07.360 --> 01:14:12.780
Our opponent played queen d6, and now we found the perfect opportunity to play knight h5,

01:14:12.780 --> 01:14:15.800
Our opponent played king h8, and now the other knight comes into e5,

01:14:15.800 --> 01:14:20.980
And already the game is over, because once again, if knight e5, then we play d5,

01:14:20.980 --> 01:14:26.920
If black takes the bishop on g5, then the open h-file only serves to further illustrate

01:14:26.920 --> 01:14:29.240
the hopelessness of black's position,

01:14:29.240 --> 01:14:35.060
In response to knight takes e5, we can again switch from positional to tactical gear,

01:14:35.060 --> 01:14:40.080
We can take with the pawn voluntarily, and the reason is to garner a tempo that we can

01:14:40.080 --> 01:14:42.680
use to stick the knight on f6,

01:14:42.680 --> 01:14:43.680
And just look at this,

01:14:43.680 --> 01:14:50.200
gf6, simply ef6, pre-moving queen h5, and the game is over,

01:14:50.200 --> 01:14:55.040
Just look at black's pathetic game, rook f7, queen h5, rook f8, g6 is I think a simple

01:14:55.040 --> 01:14:56.980
way to end the game,

01:14:56.980 --> 01:15:02.300
So that's another one of the sub-themes of this game, which is like, not assuming that

01:15:02.300 --> 01:15:04.260
you're playing with one mode, right?

01:15:04.260 --> 01:15:06.540
You're not always playing positionally or tactically,

01:15:06.540 --> 01:15:11.900
You could basically be doing both at the same time, and you're deciding on a mode based

01:15:11.900 --> 01:15:14.820
on how your opponent is reacting,

01:15:14.820 --> 01:15:19.220
But here we're harnessing both our positional advantages, like i,e, our control over the

01:15:19.220 --> 01:15:24.460
key central square, as well as our preponderance of pieces on the king side,

01:15:24.460 --> 01:15:28.660
And this kind of play is incredibly, incredibly powerful,

01:15:28.660 --> 01:15:33.060
And we conclude the game with a simple sacrifice, knight takes g7, followed by queen h5, and

01:15:33.060 --> 01:15:35.140
this is already forced mate,

01:15:35.140 --> 01:15:39.820
According to the engine, the fastest mate would have been queen h6 check, and now bishop

01:15:39.820 --> 01:15:41.180
f6, but who cares?

01:15:41.180 --> 01:15:48.380
This technically is the engine way to mate, but I think we found a completely satisfactory

01:15:48.380 --> 01:15:49.620
way to win the game,

01:15:49.620 --> 01:15:52.660
There was no doubt about the result,

01:15:52.660 --> 01:15:56.620
But according to the engine, queen h6 check, and bishop f6 is fastest, because here rook

01:15:56.620 --> 01:16:01.260
g3 is mate, and if black gives away the rook, then we play ef,

01:16:01.260 --> 01:16:06.740
I calculated to this position and forgot that we have a check on g3, followed by rook g7,

01:16:06.740 --> 01:16:08.620
and this is just mate on the spot,

01:16:08.620 --> 01:16:14.900
So this would be the fastest way to win the game, but we threw in the immediate check,

01:16:14.900 --> 01:16:20.100
And the only important line here was to understand that king takes f6 gets check mated with queen

01:16:20.100 --> 01:16:25.660
h5, again notice the power of the pawn on h4, followed by rook to f3,

01:16:25.660 --> 01:16:29.100
And that's another cool takeaway from this game, I think most people when they think

01:16:29.100 --> 01:16:35.260
about the use of pawns in attacking, they think of pawn storms,

01:16:35.260 --> 01:16:39.860
So when you see the move h4 as a less experienced player, you might think that the only idea

01:16:39.860 --> 01:16:44.380
is just to run the pawn up to h6, and to shatter black's pawn formation,

01:16:44.380 --> 01:16:48.680
And that is a viable use of pawns in attacking, but there is a second use, which is to basically

01:16:48.680 --> 01:16:53.240
advance pawns and use them as pedestals on which you can put your pieces,

01:16:53.240 --> 01:17:00.540
Like the pawn can be a very powerful supporting piece, which can open doors for your minor

01:17:00.540 --> 01:17:04.420
pieces to jump into squares that they otherwise couldn't access,

01:17:04.420 --> 01:17:09.380
And that's ultimately what ended up happening in the game, or what could have ended up happening

01:17:09.380 --> 01:17:12.240
if black had played king takes f6,

01:17:12.240 --> 01:17:16.420
As it stood, like we just played rook c3, then we take the knight, and then we mate

01:17:16.420 --> 01:17:22.140
with queen to g6, or platform is a better term I think,

01:17:22.140 --> 01:17:29.300
So int90 asks, why not the immediate rook c3 after knight c2, well because that blunders

01:17:29.300 --> 01:17:31.740
the pawn on f2, and then the rook on e1,

01:17:31.740 --> 01:17:35.860
This would be the best way to complicate the game, white is still winning after bishop

01:17:35.860 --> 01:17:38.560
takes c2, this just illustrates how bad it is for black,

01:17:38.560 --> 01:17:42.660
You can literally blunder this pawn with check and then the rook, and you're still winning,

01:17:42.660 --> 01:17:46.120
